                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harder-wearing small repairs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part polyester</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Exterior trim, broken corners</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part epoxy paste</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rot, rebuilding missing wood</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy putty stick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small strong fixes, screw holes</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is also a separate category worth knowing: <strong>grain filler</strong>. It is a thin paste used on open-grained wood such as oak before finishing to give a glass-smooth surface. Some fillers, notably Goodfilla, can be thinned to serve that purpose. Most cannot, and grain filling is not what most homeowners mean when they ask for wood filler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filler-putty-caulk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Wood Filler, Wood Putty and Caulk Are Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            These three get used interchangeably and they should not be. Picking the wrong one is behind a large share of failed repairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wood filler</strong> hardens. It is applied to bare wood, sanded flush, and then finished. Because it is rigid once dry, it belongs in holes and damage that do not move: nail holes, gouges, dents and chipped edges. <strong>Wood putty</strong> stays soft. It is usually oil-based, comes in wood tones, and goes into nail holes once the final finish is on, typically on stained trim and furniture. It is never sanded and it is not meant for bare wood under paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Caulk</strong> stays flexible. It belongs in joints between two pieces that move relative to each other, such as trim to wall, trim to siding, or window casing to frame. Using a rigid filler in a moving joint guarantees a crack as soon as the seasons change. Using caulk to fill a nail hole on a flat surface leaves a soft, slightly sunken spot that shows through paint. For exterior joints, see our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cured state</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Applied to</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sanded?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bare wood before finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Holes and damage that do not move</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood putty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Finished wood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nail holes in stained work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Caulk or sealant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joints between pieces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gaps that open and close</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A practical example: painting exterior window trim usually involves all three. Fill nail holes and dents in the casing with filler, sand and prime. Rebuild any soft sill sections with epoxy. Then caulk the joints between the casing and siding with an elastomeric sealant, following a process like the one in our <a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">exterior window caulking guide</a>. Each product handles the job it is designed for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Truth About Stainable Wood Filler
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stainable is the most overused word on wood filler labels. It usually means the filler will absorb some stain and change color. It almost never means the patch will look like the surrounding wood once stained, because real wood has grain, varying density and end-grain absorption that a uniform paste cannot reproduce.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best results come from choosing the filler color close to the final stained color, not close to the raw wood. Apply the filler, let it dry, sand it, and then stain a test board that includes both wood and filler. If the patch comes out too light, try a darker filler tone or add a touch of the stain or a compatible tint to the filler before applying. If it comes out too dark, go lighter. Minwax Stainable and Goodfilla are the easiest products to tune this way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Another reliable approach on stained work is to skip filler under the stain entirely. Stain and finish the wood first, then fill nail holes with a colored wax or oil-based putty matched to the finished surface. That is how most finish carpenters handle stained trim, and it hides small holes far better than stainable filler.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE FOR STAIN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Test on scrap, every time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Stain the scrap board with the same wood, the same filler and the same stain you plan to use, and look at it in the room's light. Ten minutes of testing beats an obvious patch in the middle of a tabletop.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two-part fillers and epoxies are paint-grade. They do not absorb stain in any meaningful way, so for stained exterior work such as a cedar fence or a stained door, either accept a visible repair or replace the damaged board. For decks and siding finished with semi-transparent stain, see our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain guide</a> for products that can help disguise minor repairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="exterior" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Exterior Repairs and Rotted Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoors, wood filler has to survive sun, water and seasonal movement. Latex fillers rated for exterior use do well on small holes once they are primed and painted, but they are vulnerable if left bare or used in large patches. For anything bigger than a nail hole on exterior trim, siding or a door, a two-part product is the more durable choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rot is a different problem altogether. Rotted wood has lost its structure, so filler applied over it has nothing solid to grip. The repair process has four steps. First, remove all wood that is soft enough to dent with a fingernail or probe with an awl, using a chisel, rasp or oscillating tool. Second, let the area dry thoroughly; moisture trapped under epoxy causes failure. Third, saturate the remaining fibers with a consolidant such as Abatron LiquidWood or Minwax Wood Hardener, which soaks in and hardens them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, rebuild with a two-part epoxy or polyester filler, overfilling slightly and shaping it close to the final profile while it is still workable. Once cured, sand or plane it flush, prime and paint. Done properly, this kind of repair can last as long as the surrounding wood. Done over damp or punky wood, it pops off within a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know when to replace instead. If rot extends through most of a board's thickness, reaches a structural member, or affects more than roughly a third of a piece of trim, replacement is often faster and more durable. Also find and fix the water source. Rot at a window sill usually means water is getting past failed caulk or a missing drip cap; rot at the bottom of a door jamb often means splashback or a missing sweep.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the repair, seal the surrounding joints with an exterior sealant and protect the wood with a sound coat of paint or a finish like those in our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a>. Filler alone will not keep water out; it only replaces what water already took.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Apply and Sand Wood Filler
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the hole first. Remove loose fibers, dust and old finish so the filler bonds to sound wood. For small holes, the corner of a putty knife works; for larger damage, square up the edges with a chisel so the filler has crisp sides to grip. Wipe away dust with a slightly damp rag and let it dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Press, do not spread. Use a flexible putty knife to force filler into the hole, then scrape across at a low angle to remove most of the excess. The goal is a slight crown above the surface, because one-part fillers shrink a little as they dry. A crown that is too tall just means more sanding; a fill that is flush or low will need a second coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fill deep holes in layers. Most one-part latex fillers are happiest at a quarter inch or less per layer. A thick plug dries on the outside but stays soft in the middle, then shrinks and cracks. Apply a layer, let it dry fully, and add another. Two-part fillers and epoxies can go in thicker, but mix only as much as you can use within the working time on the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sand once fully dry. Start with 120-grit on a sanding block for flat surfaces, which keeps the patch level with the surrounding wood rather than dishing it out. Finish with 180- or 220-grit. On trim with a profile, wrap the paper around a dowel or a profile block. Wipe off dust, check for pinholes and low spots under a raking light, and touch up as needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prime before painting. Filler absorbs paint differently than wood, and a spot-primed patch prevents flashing, the dull or shiny spot that shows through the topcoat. Use a good bonding primer outdoors, and paint within the time recommended on the filler and primer labels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drying and Recoat Times by Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drying time is one of the biggest practical differences between filler types, and it is often what decides which product people reach for. The ranges below are typical; the specific label always wins.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Times Before Sanding</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15-60 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin latex fill</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-8 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Deep latex fill</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-30 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Two-part polyester</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Hours to a day</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Two-part epoxy paste</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One-part latex fillers dry by evaporation, so time depends heavily on depth, temperature and humidity. A thin skim on a nail hole may be sandable in 15 minutes; a quarter-inch fill can take a few hours; a deep fill done in one pass can take far longer and may never cure properly in the middle. Color-change formulas such as DAP Plastic Wood-X take the guesswork out by showing when the filler is dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-part products cure by chemical reaction, not evaporation, so they cure throughout the patch at the same time. Polyester fillers tend to be the fastest, often sandable within half an hour. Epoxy pastes are slower, with working times that allow shaping and cure times ranging from several hours to overnight depending on temperature. Cold slows all two-part chemistry considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are painting trim that also needs caulking, plan the sequence so each product has time to set: fill, sand and prime first, then caulk, then paint once the sealant's paint window has passed. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">caulk drying times</a> covers that last step in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strength" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Can You Screw or Nail Into Wood Filler?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It depends on the type. One-part latex and acrylic fillers are not designed to hold fasteners. A screw driven into a latex-filled hole usually crumbles the filler and spins without biting. If a hinge screw has stripped its hole, latex filler is the wrong fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-part epoxies and epoxy putty sticks can hold screws well once fully cured. Fill the stripped hole, let it cure, then drill a pilot hole sized for the screw and drive it in. Manufacturers such as J-B Weld and Abatron describe their products as drillable and able to accept fasteners after cure. For heavy loads, such as a door hinge or a railing bracket, the traditional fix is still a solid one: glue a hardwood dowel or a few wooden toothpicks or matchsticks into the hole, trim flush and redrill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nails are a different story. Most fillers will crack if a nail is driven through them, though cured epoxy pastes can generally be nailed if you predrill. If a repair will need to be fastened, plan for it before you fill: use an epoxy, or place a wood block or dowel in the repair as an anchor.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of these products restores real structural strength to a framing member, stair tread or load-bearing part. They are surface and trim repairs. Structural damage needs replacement or reinforcement, and anything that holds weight overhead or underfoot deserves a careful look before you reach for filler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cost, Container Size and Shelf Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood filler is inexpensive compared with the time spent on a repair. At the time of writing, one-part latex and acrylic fillers run roughly $5 to $15 for a small to pint-size container. Two-part polyester kits run roughly $15 to $25. Epoxy putty sticks cost roughly $8 to $15 each, and full two-part epoxy repair systems with consolidant can run $40 to $80 or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the size you will actually use in the next year. One-part fillers harden in the container once air gets in, especially fast-drying formulas. A partly used pint that dries out has cost you more than the small tub you needed. Keep the lid clean so it seals tightly, press plastic wrap onto the surface before closing, and store containers upside down in a temperate space; freezing ruins most water-based fillers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-part products generally keep longer because the components do not react until mixed, though hardeners can degrade with age and heat. Write the purchase date on the can. Epoxy putty sticks keep well when wrapped tightly between uses. Water-based Goodfilla is unusual in that it can be revived by adding water, which reduces waste for occasional users.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where to spend: a cheap latex filler is fine for painted nail holes. For stained furniture, the extra few dollars for a stainable formula and the right color pays back in appearance. For exterior rot, buying a real epoxy system is far cheaper than paying for replacement millwork and a carpenter's time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Picking the Right Filler by Project
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are filling <strong>nail holes and dents in painted trim</strong>, a one-part latex such as DAP Plastic Wood-X or Gorilla All Purpose is all you need. If you have many holes and want to paint the same day, FamoWood's faster drying helps. Prime before painting.
          </p>

          <p>
            For <strong>stained furniture, cabinets and floors</strong>, choose Minwax Stainable or Goodfilla in a color close to your final finish, and test on scrap. For finished trim, consider colored putty after finishing instead of filler before it. For <strong>woodworking projects and open-grain woods</strong>, Goodfilla's ability to double as a grain filler is a real advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            For <strong>exterior trim, doors and siding</strong> with damage beyond a nail hole, use a two-part polyester like Minwax High Performance for fast, paint-grade repairs, or KwikWood for small, strong fixes. For <strong>rotted sills, jambs and columns</strong>, use a consolidant followed by Abatron WoodEpox, and fix the water problem that caused the rot.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the gap is a <strong>joint between two moving pieces</strong>, put the filler down and pick up a tube of exterior sealant instead. Our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk rankings</a> cover which sealant suits each kind of joint. Filler fixes holes; caulk seals gaps. Keep that straight and both last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the word stainable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stainable means the filler will take some color, not that it will vanish into the grain. Patches often come out lighter or blotchier than the wood around them. Pick a filler color close to your final stained color, test on a scrap board with the same stain, and consider colored putty after finishing for small holes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a deep hole in one pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thick plug of one-part latex filler skins on top and stays soft underneath, then shrinks and cracks as the core dries. Fill in layers of about a quarter inch, letting each dry fully, or switch to a two-part product, which hardens all the way through and does not shrink.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using filler in a joint that moves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid filler between trim and wall, or casing and siding, cracks as soon as the pieces shift with humidity and temperature. Moving joints need a flexible sealant. Use filler only in holes and damage within a single piece of wood, and caulk the joints between pieces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Patching over rot without removing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filler applied over soft, rotted wood has nothing sound to grip and traps moisture beneath it. The patch pops loose and the rot keeps spreading. Dig out all soft wood, let it dry, harden the remaining fibers with a consolidant, rebuild with epoxy, and fix the water source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving exterior filler unprimed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even exterior-rated latex filler degrades when left bare in sun and rain, and it absorbs paint differently from the wood, causing visible flashing. Spot-prime every patch with a bonding primer, then paint the whole surface. Primed and painted, small exterior fills last far longer. Recheck the patches each spring.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which filler should I use on outdoor trim and siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For small holes on painted exterior wood, DAP Plastic Wood-X performs well once primed and painted. For larger damage, broken edges or weathered trim, a two-part product such as Minwax High Performance or Abatron WoodEpox is more durable because it does not shrink and resists moisture. For rot, use a consolidant followed by epoxy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is wood putty the same as wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are different products. Filler hardens and is applied to bare wood, sanded flush and then painted or finished. Wood putty stays soft, is usually oil-based, and fills nail holes once the finish is already on, mainly on stained work. Putty is not sanded and should not be used under paint on bare wood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I make wood filler match stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a filler color close to the final stained color rather than the raw wood. Apply, dry and sand it, then stain a test board with both wood and filler. Adjust the filler tone if the patch is off. Products formulated to accept stain, such as Minwax Stainable or Goodfilla, are easier to tune than general-purpose fillers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can sand wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Thin latex fills are often sandable in 15 minutes to an hour; deep latex fills can take several hours or more. Two-part polyester fillers typically sand within about half an hour, while epoxy pastes may need several hours to overnight. Temperature and depth change everything, so follow the label and make sure the center is hard, not just the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I fill a big hole or a missing chunk of wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a two-part product rather than a one-part latex. Two-part polyester fillers and epoxy pastes cure throughout without shrinking and can be built up thick. For very large voids, glue in a block of wood first to take up most of the space, then fill around it. For rotted areas, remove all soft wood and use a consolidant before filling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a screw hold in wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in one-part latex filler, which crumbles under a screw. Cured two-part epoxy and epoxy putty sticks can hold screws if you drill a pilot hole after cure. For stripped hinge screws and other loaded fasteners, gluing a hardwood dowel or wood slivers into the hole and redrilling is still the most reliable fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can wood filler be used on decks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for small, cosmetic repairs on painted or solid-stained decks, and even then it tends to show. Deck boards move with moisture, take foot traffic and are usually finished with semi-transparent stain that will not hide filler. Badly damaged deck boards are better replaced. Use an epoxy only for small, localized damage on railings or trim.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
